      "math_academy_id": "q-28194",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-4x-2,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & -1 & -3 & -4 & -2 \\\\  &  & 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\  & -1 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+1} & =-{x}^{2}-2x-2 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+1)({x}^{2}+2x+2).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+2x+2=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={2}^{2}-4(1)(2)=-4<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny=-(0)^{3}-3(0)^{2}-4(0)-2=-2\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-2),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-1,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251864/assets/de0741fcb960e2b140660ac01307c8d6e69c3998fb79233ba8d17755c3d10fc0.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
